GFX(3)                         P32W Unix                          GFX(3)

NAME
       gfx, gopen, gline, gcircle, gtext - draw on the video screen

SYNOPSIS
       #include <gfx.h>

       int  gopen(void);
       void gsize(int w, int h);
       void gclose(void);
       int  gflush(void);
       int  gsync(void);

       void gclear(void);
       void gink(int ink);
       void gclip(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1);
       void gstyle(unsigned dashes);
       void gpattern(const unsigned short *rows);

       void gpoint(int x, int y);
       int  gget(int x, int y);
       void gline(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1);
       void gmove(int x, int y);
       void gdraw(int x, int y);
       void gpoly(const int *xy, int n);
       void gfpoly(const int *xy, int n);
       void gbox(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1);
       void gfbox(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1);
       void gcircle(int x, int y, int r);
       void gfcircle(int x, int y, int r);
       void gellipse(int x, int y, int rx, int ry);
       void gfellipse(int x, int y, int rx, int ry);
       void garc(int x, int y, int rx, int ry, int a0, int a1);
       int  gfill(int x, int y);

       void gcopy(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1, int x, int y);
       int  gkeep(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1);
       int  gmake(int w, int h);
       void gtarget(int n);
       void gstamp(int n, int x, int y, int how);
       void gdrop(int n);
       int  gread(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1,
                  unsigned short *bits);
       int  gwrite(int x0, int y0, int x1, int y1,
                   const unsigned short *bits, int how);

       void gtext(int x, int y, const char *s);
       void glook(int look);

       int  gmopen(void);
       void gmclose(void);
       int  gmouse(int *x, int *y, int *buttons, int wait);
       int  ginput(int *x, int *y, int *buttons, int wait);
       void gmat(int x, int y);
       int  gmfd(void);

       int  gsin(int degrees);
       int  gcos(int degrees);

       void gwords(const unsigned short *w, int n);
       int  gfd(void);

       extern int gwidth, gheight, gcellw, gcellh;

       cc prog.c -lgfx

DESCRIPTION
       The library draws on the video card's screen: 640 by 480
       dots, each lit or not. It works through /dev/video
       (video(4)): the card's chip does the drawing, and the
       library sends it its commands.

       gopen takes the screen and clears it. From then until
       gclose, or the end of the program, the console's text is
       not shown; afterwards it is back as if nothing had been
       drawn. One program at a time may have the screen, and only
       root and group staff.

       The library gathers what it has to send and writes it a
       buffer at a time. Nothing need show until gflush, which
       sends what has been drawn so far. gsync does the same and
       then waits until the chip has drawn it.

THE SCREEN
       x 0, y 0 is the bottom-left dot. x runs to the right, up
       to gwidth - 1 (639); y runs up, to gheight - 1 (479). A
       figure may lie partly or wholly off the screen: only what
       is on it is drawn. gclip makes that true of a smaller
       rectangle (two opposite corners, both inside it) until it
       is called again;
              gclip(0, 0, gwidth - 1, gheight - 1)
       is the whole screen again.

       gclear puts every dot out, whatever the clipping. gget
       gives 1 if a dot is lit and 0 if it is not, or is off the
       screen.

INK
       gink chooses what drawing does to a dot:

       G_WHITE   lights it (as after gopen)
       G_BLACK   puts it out
       G_XOR     turns it over: lit if it was out, out if lit

       Every routine draws each dot of its figure once. So a
       figure drawn a second time in G_XOR is gone, and what was
       under it is as it was: the way to move a thing over a
       picture (EXAMPLES). Two figures that cross, and two sides
       of an outline where they run into a sharp corner, share
       dots as any two figures do.

FIGURES
       gpoint is one dot. gline is a line with both its ends.

       gmove puts the pen somewhere, drawing nothing. gdraw draws
       a line from the pen to x, y, where the pen then is. Lines
       drawn one after another this way are joined: the dot where
       two of them meet is drawn once, and so is the first dot of
       a chain that comes back to where it began. Any other
       routine, gflush too, ends the chain.

       gpoly is the closed outline through n points: xy has the x
       and then the y of each.

       gbox is a rectangle's outline, given two opposite corners;
       gfbox is the rectangle filled.

       gcircle is a circle about x, y; gfcircle is it filled.
       gellipse and gfellipse are an ellipse rx to each side of
       its centre and ry above and below it. The dots are square,
       so a circle is round. A radius may be up to 16383.

       garc is the part of that ellipse from the direction a0
       round to a1: degrees as seen from the centre, 0 to the
       right, 90 up, anticlockwise. With a1 less than a0 the arc
       still runs anticlockwise, through 0; with a1 a whole turn
       or more past a0 it is all of the ellipse.

STYLES AND PATTERNS
       gstyle gives lines a style: 16 dots, a 1 a dot drawn and a
       0 one left as it is, taken over and over along the line
       from its first dot, the top bit first. 0xF0F0 is dashes of
       four dots, 0xAAAA every other dot. It is for gline, gdraw,
       gpoly and gbox; along gdraw's joined lines, and round a
       box, it runs on. gstyle(0xFFFF) is whole lines again.

       gpattern gives filled figures a pattern: 16 rows of 16
       dots, rows[0] the top row, a row's top bit its left-hand
       dot. Again a 1 is a dot drawn (lit, put out or turned
       over, as the ink says) and a 0 one left as it is. The
       pattern is laid over the whole screen from its top-left
       corner, and a figure shows the part it covers: so figures
       that touch fit together. It is for gfbox, gfcircle,
       gfellipse, gfpoly and gfill. gpattern(0) is solid again.

FILLED OUTLINES
       gfpoly fills the closed outline through n points (256 at
       most, each coordinate from -16383 to 16383): every dot
       inside the outline or on it, so that the outline through
       a box's four corners is gfbox. Where an outline crosses
       itself, a place is inside if a line from it to far away
       crosses the outline an odd number of times: a star drawn
       in five strokes has a hole in its middle.

       gfill fills an area of the picture as it stands: the dot
       x, y and every dot joined to it (above, below, left or
       right) that is as it is, lit or not, as far as the
       clipping rectangle. After
              gcircle(100, 100, 40); gfill(100, 100);
       the circle is filled, and gfill(0, 0) would fill all the
       rest of the screen. It returns 0, or -1 if the screen
       could not be read or the area has more corners than
       gfill can hold (what it reached is filled).

PIECES OF THE PICTURE
       gcopy copies the rectangle with the corners x0, y0 and
       x1, y1 to where its bottom-left dot is x, y. The two
       places may overlap. What is copied is cut to the screen,
       and where it lands to the clipping rectangle.

       gkeep keeps a copy of a rectangle off the screen, in the
       chip's own memory, and returns its number (0 or more), or
       -1 if there is no room: 64 pictures at most, and about
       five screens of dots in all. gstamp puts picture n down
       with its bottom-left dot at x, y:

       G_COPY    as it is, its unlit dots too
       G_OVER    its lit dots added to what is there
       G_MASK    only the dots stay lit that are lit in both
       G_FLIP    its lit dots turn over what is there: put down
                 there a second time, it is gone

       gdrop forgets a picture: its room is used for another.
       A kept picture takes a few words to draw, whatever is in
       it. That is the way to move a shape about: keep it once,
       G_FLIP it where it is to be, and again to take it away.
       The pictures are gone when the screen is given back.

       gmake makes a kept picture of w by h dots with nothing in
       it, and gtarget(n) sends all drawing into kept picture n
       instead of onto the screen: its bottom-left dot is then
       0, 0, gwidth and gheight are its size, and nothing is
       drawn outside it. gtarget(-1) is the screen again; either
       way the clipping rectangle is the whole of what is drawn
       on. So a picture can be made out of sight and put down
       whole with gstamp, without its being seen growing; and
       gget, gfill, gread and gkeep work in a kept picture as
       they do on the screen.

       gread and gwrite take the dots through the program
       instead, for a picture that goes to a file or comes from
       one. gread puts the rectangle's dots into bits: its top
       line first, each line in (width + 15) / 16 words, a word's
       top bit its left-hand dot; a dot off the screen is 0.
       gwrite draws such bits in that rectangle, with how as for
       gstamp, inside the clipping rectangle. Both return 0, or
       -1 if the device could not be read or written.

IN A WINDOW
       Under wm(1) the screen is the window manager's. A program
       run in one of its windows draws all the same: gopen asks
       wm for a window for the picture, and every call here is
       sent to wm, which does the drawing. The program need not
       know. Its picture is 400 by 300 dots; or the size the
       program asked for with gsize before gopen; or, before
       either, what the variable WMSIZE says (as 320x200). It is
       640 by 440 at most. gwidth and gheight are its size, so a
       program that goes by them fits. On the screen itself
       gsize does nothing.

       What differs: nothing shows until gflush, as always, but
       there it matters more; gmake, gtarget, gread and gwords
       do nothing (gmake and gread return -1). And a call that
       returns something (gget, gfill, gkeep, gsync, gmouse,
       ginput with the mouse) has its answer typed back at the
       program's terminal: the program must be the one the
       terminal is listening to, not one started with &: or be
       started with win(1), which gives it a terminal of its
       own. A program that only draws may be started any way.
       gmouse and ginput there tell where the pointer is in the
       picture, and the buttons while they are pressed in it.
       The keys typed while the picture's window is in front are
       the program's: ginput hands them over, where a read of
       the terminal would also find wm's answers.

TEXT
       gtext draws s in the console's font, each character gcellw
       (8) dots wide and gcellh (16) high; x, y is the bottom-left
       dot of the first one's cell. A newline in s starts again
       at x, one cell lower. A character the font has not (the
       printable ASCII ones are all it has) is drawn as ?.

       The ink says what happens to the dots of the letters: lit,
       put out, or turned over. The rest of each cell is left as
       it was. glook changes the look, from then on; add together

       G_BOLD     bold
       G_UNDER    underlined
       G_OPAQUE   the rest of each cell is drawn too, in the other
                  colour: white letters on black, or with
                  G_BLACK black letters on white. Text written
                  this way covers what was there, other text
                  too.

       glook(0) is plain again.

THE MOUSE
       gmopen takes the mouse (mouse(4)) and puts its pointer at
       the middle of the screen; gmclose gives it back. gmouse
       tells where the pointer is, a place on the screen that
       the mouse's movements are added up into and that stops at
       the screen's edges, and which buttons are down: 1 the
       left, 2 the right, 4 the middle, added together. With wait
       0 it tells how things are now; with wait above 0 it first
       waits up to that many 50ths of a second for the mouse to
       move or a button to change; below 0, until one does. It
       returns 1 if anything is different since the last call, 0
       if not, and -1 if the mouse cannot be read.

       A button that goes down or comes up is never passed over:
       gmouse returns with that change, and what the mouse did
       after it waits for the next call. So a click is seen
       however seldom a program asks.

       ginput waits for the mouse and the keyboard at once, as a
       program does that is worked with both. It waits as gmouse
       does and returns what came first: a key that was typed,
       as its character (1 to 255); G_MOUSE when the mouse moved
       or a button changed, with x, y and buttons as gmouse
       gives them; 0 if neither did in the time, or a signal the
       program catches came; -1 if neither can be read (the
       terminal is gone). Without gmopen it is the keys alone,
       and wait is then a way to do something every so often.
       What has been drawn is sent first, as by gflush. From the
       first ginput until gclose the terminal gives each key as
       it is typed, without showing it: an arrow comes as its
       three characters, ESC [ and a letter.

       gmat puts the pointer somewhere. gmfd is the mouse's file
       descriptor, for a program that waits for the mouse and
       something else of its own with select(2).

       The library draws no pointer: a program draws its own and
       moves it when gmouse says so. A kept picture put down with
       G_FLIP, or lines in G_XOR, can be taken away again by
       drawing them a second time (EXAMPLES). Any of x, y and
       buttons may be 0 if it is not wanted. The mouse does not
       need the screen: without gopen the pointer starts at 0, 0
       and has no edges to stop at above and to the right.

SINES
       gsin and gcos give the sine and cosine of a whole number
       of degrees, any number, multiplied by G_ONE (16384): for
       turning things without floating point. The point d from
       x, y in the direction a is
              x + d * gcos(a) / G_ONE,  y + d * gsin(a) / G_ONE

THE CHIP ITSELF
       gwords sends n of the chip's own command words (video(4)),
       in their turn among what the library sends. gfd gives the
       file descriptor of /dev/video, to read what such a command
       reads: call gflush first. The library depends on the
       chip's origin and mask being as it finds them, and on the
       colour registers 0 and 1, the pattern RAM, the pattern's
       pointer (register 5) and the area registers 8 to 0x0B
       being as it left them: a program that changes one of
       these puts it back. gkeep's pictures are in the lines of
       the chip's memory that VIOCSPARE names (video(4)).

RETURN VALUE
       gopen returns 0, or -1 with errno set as open(2) sets it:
       ENXIO no video card, EBUSY another program has the screen,
       EACCES not allowed. gflush and gsync return 0, or -1 if
       something could not be written since the last time (or
       the screen is not open).

EXAMPLES
       /usr/examples/clock.c
              a clock: its face drawn once, its hands in G_XOR
       /usr/examples/mouse.c
              a cross that follows the mouse, and draws
       /usr/src/cmd/clock.c, view.c, files.c
              the desk's programs (clock(1), view(1), files(1)):
              gsize, ginput for keys and the mouse, a picture
              from a file with gwrite

       #include <gfx.h>
       #include <unistd.h>

       int main(void)
       {
           if (gopen() < 0) return 1;
           gbox(0, 0, gwidth - 1, gheight - 1);
           gcircle(320, 240, 100);
           gtext(304, 232, "P32W");
           gflush();
           sleep(5);
           gclose();
           return 0;
       }

       cc -o ring ring.c -lgfx

FILES
       /usr/lib/libgfx.a, /usr/include/gfx.h, /dev/video,
       /dev/mouse
       /usr/src/libgfx    its source

SEE ALSO
       wm(1), win(1), video(4), mouse(4), cc(1)

LIMITATIONS
       Black and white only. A style is for straight lines:
       circles and arcs are always whole. gfill has to read the
       screen, 128 dots at a time, to find its area, where the
       chip's own PAINT command would not (the emulators have
       no PAINT, and nothing here uses a command that has not
       been run on the real chip): a circle of radius 100 takes
       two seconds to fill, the whole screen ten. gread and
       gwrite of the whole screen take seven and five.

       The library works out a circle's dots itself and has the
       chip draw them as short runs, so that they are the same
       on any chip and each is drawn once; the chip's own circle
       command would be faster. Which dots a slanted line has is
       the chip's choice.

       An ellipse far larger than the screen (not a circle) is
       drawn to fewer places, and may be a dot out here and
       there.
